WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:06.910
An automation never gets tired, never complains, and never asks if anyone reads what it sends.

00:00:07.780 --> 00:00:09.370
And that's how it goes wrong.

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:15.650
A weekly summary keeps arriving in Slack, right on time, and nobody acts on it.

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:20.650
It's like a radio that keeps playing long after everyone left the house.

00:00:21.140 --> 00:00:23.390
Except every run costs tokens,

00:00:23.780 --> 00:00:28.370
and an unchecked one can put wrong information on a board your team trusts.

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:34.590
That's because an automation runs while you're elsewhere, with nobody to answer its questions.

00:00:35.080 --> 00:00:39.570
So you decide first what starts it, how often, and what it reads.

00:00:40.180 --> 00:00:42.790
Then you prove it by hand, three times.

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:48.710
That's why, in this adventure, you keep, fix or stop what you already run,

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:52.570
then build one automation properly, and keep a checklist.

00:00:53.080 --> 00:00:54.790
You're done when a run arrives,

00:00:54.940 --> 00:00:56.950
and someone acts on it.
